# Proposed fuzz-test approach for `src/lex.zig`

No code written.

## 1. Fuzz the public ABI first

The main fuzz target should exercise the exported engine API in [`src/lex.zig`](src/lex.zig#L4028-L4198), not the private `State` methods.

Reason: the production caller allocates raw state, initializes via `lex_init`, feeds `lex_add`, uses synthetic backspaces/replacement, and guards backspace/full states through the ABI. That flow is visible in the Swift wrapper: reset/add/backspace wrappers in [`Lex.swift`](macos/Lex.swift#L64-L99), and the event-path add/replacement sequence in [`Lex.swift`](macos/Lex.swift#L520-L586).

A private `State` fuzz target can come later if we want internal invariants such as exact `literal_index`, span contents, or `buffer_modification_index`.

## 2. Fuzz operation streams, not arbitrary API calls

The fuzzer should generate a stream of valid caller-level operations:

- add one ASCII alphabetic character;
- backspace only when not empty;
- reset/reinitialize, because production uses `lex_init` as reset;
- query booleans at any time;
- calculate synthetic backspaces and compose replacement only after an add on the synthetic replacement path;
- avoid calling `lex_add` after `lex_buffer_full` without first resetting, matching the caller;
- avoid `lex_compose_utf16_string_replacement` when the effective buffer is already full, because composition asserts `buffer_length <= buffer_effective.len` internally at [`src/lex.zig`](src/lex.zig#L1826-L1849).

This matters because `lex_add` and other functions intentionally use assertions for documented preconditions, e.g. alphabetic-only input in [`State.add`](src/lex.zig#L630-L647). The fuzz target should find engine bugs, not expected assertion failures from invalid caller behavior.

## 3. Maintain an external visible-text model

The strongest ABI-level oracle is a small external model of what the caller would see:

- On normal synthetic-path add:
  - call `lex_add`;
  - get synthetic backspace count;
  - remove that many UTF-16 units from the model;
  - compose replacement;
  - append the replacement units.
- On effective-buffer-full pass-through add:
  - call `lex_add`;
  - append the original ASCII input to the model;
  - do not calculate replacement.
- On real backspace:
  - remove one visible UTF-16 unit from the model if present;
  - call `lex_backspace`.

Then assert structural properties:

- no model underflow;
- synthetic backspaces never exceed visible length;
- replacement count is `<= lex_replacement_buffer_length`;
- replacement buffer writes do not exceed `replacement_count`;
- untouched sentinel slots remain untouched;
- replacement code units are either ASCII alphabetic or independently enumerated Vietnamese BMP code points allowed by the spec;
- after reset or backspacing to empty, subsequent behavior matches a fresh state.

This is better than “no crash” alone because it tests the app-facing synthetic replacement contract implemented around [`calculate_synthetic_backspaces`](src/lex.zig#L1812-L1824) and replacement composition.

## 4. Seed corpus should target spec boundaries

The curated corpus should be operation-level, not only strings. It should emphasize:

- literal starts and literal tails for `F/J/W/Z`, per [`specs`](specs/lex-vietnamese-telex-input-method.md#L7-L16);
- diacritic apply/override/cancel, especially literal transition after cancel, per [`specs`](specs/lex-vietnamese-telex-input-method.md#L72-L83);
- autofill `uơ` / `ưo` with `C/I/M/N/P/T/U` and `QU` exclusions, per [`specs`](specs/lex-vietnamese-telex-input-method.md#L85-L94);
- tone apply/cancel/reset/override for `S/F/R/X/J/Z`, per [`specs`](specs/lex-vietnamese-telex-input-method.md#L138-L189);
- pseudoword and tone placement boundaries: `GI`, `QU`, `OA`, `OE`, `OO`, `UY`, per [`specs`](specs/lex-vietnamese-telex-input-method.md#L191-L224);
- buffer effective boundaries at 15/16/17;
- full `u8` buffer-length boundary near 254/255, with reset before adding past full;
- backspaces around `literal_index`.

## 5. Build integration should stay minimal

Current `build.zig` already creates a host test artifact for [`src/lex.zig`](build.zig#L30-L38). I would initially put the fuzz test behind the normal test artifact and rely on:

- ordinary `zig build test` for deterministic corpus execution;
- `zig build test --fuzz` for actual fuzzing.

A separate `fuzz` build step is only worth adding if we need a different optimize mode, test filter, timeout, target, or named workflow.

## 6. Zig 0.16 API needs one explicit decision before implementation

There is conflicting information:

- official 0.16 release notes mention a Smith-based fuzz interface;
- inspecting `ziglang/zig` tag `0.16.0` via Librarian reported `std.testing.fuzz` still taking a `[]const u8` callback and `FuzzInputOptions` containing only `corpus`.

Oracle argued to plan around Smith and warned not to implement against raw bytes until the compiler is confirmed.

My recommendation: before coding, pin the exact Zig 0.16.0 binary we will use and inspect its `std.testing.fuzz` declaration locally. Then adapt only the input-generation layer:

- if callback input is raw bytes, decode bytes into operations;
- if callback input is Smith, generate operations directly.

The rest of the design is unchanged.

## Oracle perspective summary

Oracle agreed with the main direction: public-ABI fuzzing, operation streams, curated boundary corpus, and simple build integration. Its useful corrections were:

- mirror the Swift caller’s precondition-gated flow exactly;
- include reset/reinit operations;
- maintain an external visible-text model;
- include 255-length buffer behavior, not only 16-span effective-buffer behavior;
- verify the Zig 0.16 fuzz API before writing code.

